      "writtenOn": "2026-06-05T18:14:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Gemini steps to implement this:\n\n-----\n\nTo fix the missing flash boundary checks, you need to validate that the requested `offset` and `size` do not exceed the physical capacity of the flash chip (`EgisBootrom::kFlashTotalSize`).\n\nBecause `offset` and `size` are both unsigned 32-bit integers, it\u0027s also crucial to write the check in a way that prevents **integer overflow**. If you just check `offset + size \u003e kFlashTotalSize`, a very large `size` could wrap around past zero and bypass the check.\n\nHere are the step-by-step changes to add robust boundary checks.\n\n### 1. Add a new error code to `bootrom.h`\n\nOpen `util/egis/bootrom.h` and add a specific error for invalid addressing.\n\nAdd `kInvalidFlashAddress` to `BootromError`:\n\n```cpp\nenum class BootromError {\n  kUsbWriteFailed,\n  // ... existing errors ...\n  kBufferTooSmall,\n  kInvalidFlashAddress, // \u003c-- Add this\n  kUnknown,\n};\n\n```\n\nUpdate the `ToString` helper in the same file:\n\n```cpp\n    case BootromError::kBufferTooSmall:\n      return \"Provided buffer is too small for serialization\";\n    case BootromError::kInvalidFlashAddress: // \u003c-- Add this block\n      return \"Flash offset and size exceed total flash capacity\";\n    default:\n      return \"An unknown error occurred\";\n\n```\n\n### 2. Add an overflow-safe helper to `bootrom.cc`\n\nOpen `util/egis/bootrom.cc`. At the top of the file, right after the `using namespace std::chrono_literals;`, add an anonymous namespace with a small helper function.\n\nChecking `size \u003c\u003d Total - offset` mathematically guarantees that the values won\u0027t overflow the 32-bit limit while validating the boundary.\n\n```cpp\nnamespace egis {\n\nusing namespace std::chrono_literals;\n\nnamespace {\nbool IsValidFlashRange(uint32_t offset, size_t size) {\n  return offset \u003c\u003d EgisBootrom::kFlashTotalSize \u0026\u0026\n         size \u003c\u003d (EgisBootrom::kFlashTotalSize - offset);\n}\n}  // namespace\n\n```\n\n### 3. Secure the Flash Operations in `bootrom.cc`\n\nNow, apply this helper to the four public methods that interact with flash memory boundaries.\n\n**BackupFlash:**\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, BootromError\u003e BootromComm::BackupFlash(\n    std::string_view filename, uint32_t total_size) {\n  if (!IsValidFlashRange(0, total_size)) {\n    std::println(stderr, \"[ERROR] Backup size {} exceeds flash capacity {}\",\n                 total_size, EgisBootrom::kFlashTotalSize);\n    return std::unexpected(BootromError::kInvalidFlashAddress);\n  }\n  \n  std::ofstream outfile(std::string(filename), ...\n\n```\n\n**ReadFlash:**\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003cstd::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e, BootromError\u003e BootromComm::ReadFlash(\n    uint32_t offset, uint32_t length) {\n  if (!IsValidFlashRange(offset, length)) {\n    std::println(stderr, \"[ERROR] Read range (offset: 0x{:X}, size: 0x{:X}) exceeds flash capacity.\",\n                 offset, length);\n    return std::unexpected(BootromError::kInvalidFlashAddress);\n  }\n\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e buffer;\n\n```\n\n**EraseFlash:**\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, BootromError\u003e BootromComm::EraseFlash(uint32_t offset,\n                                                          uint32_t size) {\n  if (!IsValidFlashRange(offset, size)) {\n    std::println(stderr, \"[ERROR] Erase range (offset: 0x{:X}, size: 0x{:X}) exceeds flash capacity.\",\n                 offset, size);\n    return std::unexpected(BootromError::kInvalidFlashAddress);\n  }\n\n  std::println(\"--- Executing CMD_FLASH_ERASE (0x22) ---\");\n\n```\n\n**WriteFlash:**\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, BootromError\u003e BootromComm::WriteFlash(\n    uint32_t offset, std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e data) {\n  if (!IsValidFlashRange(offset, data.size())) {\n    std::println(stderr, \"[ERROR] Write range (offset: 0x{:X}, size: 0x{:X}) exceeds flash capacity.\",\n                 offset, data.size());\n    return std::unexpected(BootromError::kInvalidFlashAddress);\n  }\n\n  if (data.size() \u003e kMaxFlashWriteSize) {\n    // ... existing code ...\n\n```\n\nThese checks will now gracefully reject any malformed inputs (either from a bad file size or bad CLI arguments) before issuing out-of-bounds operations to the embedded bootrom.\n\n------\n\nYou should add unit tests to `util/egis/bootrom_test.cc` to ensure these new checks correctly block out-of-bounds requests and integer overflows.\n\nYou can drop these tests directly into `util/egis/bootrom_test.cc` (at the bottom, just before the closing namespaces):\n\n```cpp\nTEST_F(BootromCommTest, WriteFlashOutOfBounds) {\n  // Try to write 200 bytes starting 100 bytes from the end of the flash\n  const uint32_t offset \u003d EgisBootrom::kFlashTotalSize - 100;\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e payload(200, 0xAA);\n\n  auto res \u003d brom_.WriteFlash(offset, payload);\n\n  ASSERT_FALSE(res.has_value());\n  EXPECT_EQ(res.error(), BootromError::kInvalidFlashAddress);\n}\n\nTEST_F(BootromCommTest, ReadFlashOutOfBounds) {\n  // Try to read exactly starting at the limit (which is 1 byte out of bounds)\n  const uint32_t offset \u003d EgisBootrom::kFlashTotalSize;\n  const uint32_t size \u003d 1;\n\n  auto res \u003d brom_.ReadFlash(offset, size);\n\n  ASSERT_FALSE(res.has_value());\n  EXPECT_EQ(res.error(), BootromError::kInvalidFlashAddress);\n}\n\nTEST_F(BootromCommTest, EraseFlashOutOfBounds) {\n  // Try to erase more than the total flash size\n  const uint32_t offset \u003d 0x1000;\n  const uint32_t size \u003d EgisBootrom::kFlashTotalSize;\n\n  auto res \u003d brom_.EraseFlash(offset, size);\n\n  ASSERT_FALSE(res.has_value());\n  EXPECT_EQ(res.error(), BootromError::kInvalidFlashAddress);\n}\n\nTEST_F(BootromCommTest, FlashAddressIntegerOverflowProtection) {\n  // Provide values that would wrap around a 32-bit unsigned integer.\n  // 0xFFFFFFF0 + 0x20 \u003d 0x00000010. If the overflow isn\u0027t caught safely, \n  // 0x10 is less than kFlashTotalSize, and the check would improperly pass.\n  const uint32_t offset \u003d 0xFFFFFFF0;\n  const uint32_t size \u003d 0x20;\n\n  auto res \u003d brom_.ReadFlash(offset, size);\n\n  ASSERT_FALSE(res.has_value());\n  EXPECT_EQ(res.error(), BootromError::kInvalidFlashAddress);\n}\n\n```\n\n### Why these tests are important:\n\n1. **The Edge Cases:** They test writing across the boundary, reading exactly on the boundary, and erasing past the boundary.\n2. **The Overflow Trap:** The `FlashAddressIntegerOverflowProtection` test explicitly verifies the overflow logic we used in the `IsValidFlashRange` helper. In C/C++, a naive `if (offset + size \u003e total)` check would succumb to this wrap-around exploit, allowing bad data to be passed down to the USB layer.",
